📊 Performance Testing Metrics – Simple Explanation Table
Metric Name	What It Means (Simple)	Why It's Important	What You Look For
Average Response Time	On average, how long does a request or transaction take to complete?	Helps measure general speed and user experience	Lower = better. Watch for spikes under load.
Median Response Time	Middle value — 50% of requests were faster than this.	More realistic than average (ignores outliers)	Closer to average = stable system.
90th / 95th / 99th Percentile	X% of responses were faster than this value. Shows worst-case performance.	Measures performance for slower users	Should not be too much higher than average.
Throughput (MB/sec or req/sec)	How much data or how many requests are processed per second.	Shows how much load the system can handle	Should go up with users; drop = bottleneck.
Hits per Second	Total HTTP requests being sent per second	Measures system pressure from users	Higher = more load. Drops = threads failing.
Transactions per Second	Number of full business actions completed per second (e.g., login, search)	Measures how fast the system completes real work	Should scale with users. Drops = performance issue.
Error %	Percentage of failed transactions or requests	Indicates stability	Should be 0% or very low (<1%).
Standard Deviation	How spread out the response times are	Measures consistency	Lower = consistent performance.
Response Codes (200, 302, 401, 500)	HTTP status codes from the server	Tells you if requests are successful or failing	Track for errors (401 = auth issue, 500 = crash)
🧭 What You’re Actually Looking For During Performance Testing:
Goal	What to Watch
✅ Stable performance	Average + median response times stay flat
✅ High capacity	Throughput and hits/sec scale with users
⚠️ No degradation	90th/95th percentile doesn't spike
🚨 Detect bottlenecks	Throughput drops, errors rise
🛑 Catch failures early	HTTP 5xx / 401 / timeouts
🧠 Demo Tip:
“We don’t just want to know how fast it is — we want to know how well it handles more users, and whether it starts failing or slowing down under load. These metrics help us see exactly when and where it breaks.”







